# Branching strategy

Phases and their order are defined in `project-overview.mdc`. Don't redefine them here; refer to them by the names and numbers used there.

## Transition

Phases 1-2 were developed before this strategy existed. Until Phase 2 is merged to `main`, keep working on the current branch and ignore the rules below about branch structure, flow, and direct commits. The strategy applies in full from Phase 3: the first release branch under it is `release/phase-3-multiplayer`, cut from `main` after Phase 2 is merged.

After Phase 2 is completed and merged to `main`, the agent may remove this Transition section or mark it completed (ask before choosing to remove this section or choosing to mark it completed).

## Structure

- `main` holds completed phases only.
- Each phase has one **release branch**: `release/phase-<n>-<short-name>`, using the phase number and a short name from `project-overview.mdc`. Examples: `release/phase-3-multiplayer`, `release/phase-4-game-mechanics`. Cut from `main` when the phase starts.
- All work happens on **feature branches** cut from the current phase's release branch: `feature/<short-description>`.
- Keep each feature branch to one focused change.
- No hotfix branches for now. Ship only through the phase release flow.

## Flow

1. Cut `release/phase-<n>-<short-name>` from `main` at the start of a phase.
2. Cut `feature/<short-description>` from that release branch.
3. When the feature is done, the agent may ask to open a pull request into the release branch; the user merges. Tests must pass first (`npm test`; later CI when present).
4. Merge the feature into the release branch with a **squash merge** (one squash commit per feature on the release branch). Delete the feature branch afterward and don't reuse it; start a new branch for follow-up work.
5. When the phase is complete, merge the release branch into `main` with a **merge commit** (no squash), so each feature's squash commit stays visible in `main`'s history and remains individually revertable.
6. Cut the next phase's release branch from `main` only after the previous phase has been merged.

## Merge style

- Feature into release: squash merge, one clean commit per feature.
- Release into main: merge commit, no squash, so features remain traceable and revertable individually.
- The user performs merges. The agent may ask to open PRs when appropriate; do not merge unless asked.

## Agent rules

- Never commit directly to `main` or any `release/*` branch.
- Never force-push, rewrite history on shared branches, or delete branches without asking.
- Don't create, merge, or delete branches unless asked. When asked to start work, confirm which phase and release branch to base it on.
- If the current branch isn't a `feature/*` branch, say so before making changes and ask which branch to use.
